VIOLATION — `user-flow-validation.md` MUST-8's own subject matter plus `rule-authoring.md`. This
rule artifact licenses the exact failure MUST-8 exists to close, and it does so while carrying
every piece of structural furniture a compliant rule carries.

Substantive defects. It conditions gate sufficiency on whether the REAL published artifact was
driven rather than on whether the gate entered from the consumer's own arrival state, so seeding
the consumer-supplied config is re-labelled "setup rather than substitution" — which is MUST-8's
"seeding the config is just test setup" BLOCKED rationalization promoted to the rule's own
premise. It then states outright that a good-versus-bad pass over the seeded state "is ordinarily
good evidence the integration works" and that the gate "may report it verified on that basis",
which is MUST-8's prohibition inverted into a permission. Finally it makes the cold entry, the
provider variants and the boundary paths a stakes-gated option that "should generally be
skipped", when those three are precisely the sub-clauses (a)/(b)/(c) MUST-8 mandates
unconditionally.

Authoring defects, per `rule-authoring.md`. The section is titled "MUST Rules" but the primary
modal throughout is "should" / "may" / "generally", so the clause is unenforceable as written.
The DO/DO-NOT block teaches nothing — "reach the integration quickly / do not over-verify"
restates the heading rather than showing two invocations that differ in outcome. The
BLOCKED-rationalizations list is abstract paraphrase ("being excessively thorough", "adding
unnecessary process overhead") rather than the verbatim phrases an agent would actually produce,
so it cannot be matched against a transcript. The **Why:** line asserts a CI-budget cost and
closes with "most integrations are fine", which is the belief under test rather than a
consequence of breaching the clause.

The Wiring block is present and well-formed with all eight fields; its `advisory` gate-review
severity follows from the rule's own inverted premise rather than being a separate defect.
